data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The French Revolution (1789–1799) was one of the most","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" pivotal events in human history. It was not a single event but a decade of systemic collapse and violent rebirth that dismantled the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" centuries-old absolute monarchy in France and replaced it with ideals of citizenship and inalienable rights. The revolution was driven","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" by a volatile mix of social inequality, economic desperation, and intellectual awakening.\n\n### Causes of the Revolution\n\nThe causes","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of the French Revolution can be categorized into social, economic, and intellectual factors.\n\n**Social Inequality: The Three Estates","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"**\nFrance was organized under the *Ancien Régime*, a rigid social hierarchy divided into three estates. The First","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Estate (the clergy) and the Second Estate (the nobility) owned the vast majority of the land and held all political","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" power, yet they were largely exempt from taxes. The Third Estate—comprising everyone from wealthy lawyers and merchants (the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" bourgeoisie) to urban laborers and rural peasants—bore the entire tax burden. This created a deep-seated resentment, as","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the majority of the population had no voice in how the country was governed.\n\n**Economic Crisis and Famine**\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"By the late 1780s, France was on the brink of bankruptcy. Decades of lavish spending by","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the monarchy and expensive foreign wars—most notably France's support of the American Revolution—had drained the treasury. To","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" make matters worse, a series of poor harvests led to severe food shortages. The price of bread, the staple diet of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the poor, skyrocketed, leading to widespread famine and \"bread riots\" in the streets of Paris. The inability of King","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Louis XVI to implement meaningful tax reform, due to resistance from the nobility, pushed the state toward collapse.\n\n**The","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Enlightenment**\nWhile the peasants were starving, the educated bourgeoisie were reading. The Enlightenment brought a wave of new ideas from","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" philosophers like Voltaire, Rousseau, and Montesquieu. They challenged the \"Divine Right of Kings\" and argued for reason","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", meritocracy, and the \"social contract.\" These ideas provided the intellectual framework for the revolution, transforming vague grievances into","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a coherent demand for liberty, equality, and fraternity.\n\n### The Course of the Revolution\nThe tension peaked in ","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"1789 when Louis XVI summoned the Estates-General to solve the financial crisis. When the Third Estate was sidelined","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", they broke away to form the National Assembly, vowing not to disperse until they had written a constitution. The storm","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"ing of the Bastille on July 14, 1789, signaled that the revolution would not be","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" merely political, but violent and popular. This led to the abolition of feudalism and the drafting of the *Declaration of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the Rights of Man and of the Citizen*. However, the revolution eventually spiraled into the \"Reign of Terror\"","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" under Robespierre, where thousands of perceived \"enemies of the revolution,\" including the King and Queen, were executed by","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" guillotine.\n\n### Consequences of the Revolution\n\nThe consequences of the French Revolution were profound, altering the trajectory of modern","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" politics.\n\n**The End of Feudalism and Absolute Monarchy**\nThe most immediate result was the destruction of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the feudal system. The legal privileges of the nobility and clergy were abolished, and the concept of the \"subject\" was","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" replaced by the \"citizen.\" Although France would fluctuate between republics, empires, and restored monarchies for decades, the era","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of absolute, unquestioned royal power was over.\n\n**The Rise of Napoleon Bonaparte**\nThe chaos and instability of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the revolutionary government created a power vacuum. This allowed Napoleon Bonaparte, a brilliant military general, to seize power in 1","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"799. While Napoleon eventually crowned himself Emperor, he preserved many revolutionary gains. His \"Napoleonic Code\"","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" standardized laws, protected property rights, and ensured legal equality for all male citizens, exporting these revolutionary ideals across Europe through his","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" conquests.\n\n**Secularism and the State**\nThe Revolution fundamentally changed the relationship between church and state. The","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Catholic Church, once the most powerful institution in France, lost its land and its monopoly on education and social control. This","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" laid the groundwork for *laïcité* (secularism), a core tenet of the modern French Republic","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":".\n\n**Global Influence and Nationalism**\nThe French Revolution ignited a global fire. Its slogans of \"Liberté,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Égalité, Fraternité\" inspired democratic movements worldwide, from the Haitian Revolution to the independence movements in Latin America","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". It also birthed modern nationalism; the idea that a nation is not the property of a king, but a collective","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" body of citizens bound by shared laws and identity.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe French Revolution was a paradox: it began with","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a quest for human rights and ended in a reign of terror and military dictatorship. However, its long-term legacy is","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" undeniable. By challenging the traditional order, it established the blueprint for the modern liberal state, asserting that government exists to serve","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the people, and that all citizens are equal before the law.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

